Make GDAL's own Python bindings buildable without a runnable `gdal-config`,
by letting the environment answer every question setup.py would have asked it.

The bindings' setup.py resolves the native library through
`gdal_ext.get_gdal_config(option)`, which calls `fetch_config()` — a
`subprocess.Popen(["gdal-config", "--<option>"])`. Three options are asked
for: `version` (to check that libgdal is at least as new as the bindings),
`prefix` (whose `lib/` and `include/` are appended to the link and compile
paths when nothing else supplied them) and `cflags`.

None of that survives a cross build. `recipes/flet-libgdal/build.sh` ends
with `rm -rf $PREFIX/{bin,share}`, so the flet-libgdal wheel ships headers
and libraries under `{platlib}/opt` and no `gdal-config`; `fetch_config`
raises `gdal_config_error`, which `get_gdal_config` re-raises with
"Could not find gdal-config. Make sure you have installed the GDAL native
library and development headers.", failing `finalize_options` at its
`prefix` call. (The `version` call is wrapped in its own
`except gdal_config_error: pass`, so a missing config tool silently skips
the bindings-vs-library version guard as well.) Should the build host happen
to carry a Homebrew or system `gdal-config`, the call succeeds instead and
hands the cross compile the host's macOS prefix — worse, because it builds
green.

The hunk short-circuits `get_gdal_config`: an option is looked up as
`GDAL_<OPTION>` in the environment first, and only falls through to the
subprocess when that variable is absent. The naming is mechanical, so the
recipe's `script_env` answers all three calls — `GDAL_VERSION: 3.13.1`,
`GDAL_PREFIX: {platlib}/opt`, `GDAL_CFLAGS: ''`. The empty `GDAL_CFLAGS` is
deliberate rather than an oversight: it is present (so no subprocess runs)
but falsy (so `if cflags:` adds nothing), which is exactly "no extra compile
flags, and don't go looking".

The libraries to link need no hook: setup.py's own `libraries = ['gdal']` is
right on both platforms, because libgdal is a shared library that resolves its
own dependencies.

This recipe is pinned to the exact `flet-libgdal` version, so a bump here is
never a lone edit.

Rationale: the hunk arrived in the bulk consolidation commit ec8d184 with no
recorded reason; the account above is read off the diff, GDAL 3.13.1's
setup.py, and the `GDAL_VERSION`/`GDAL_PREFIX`/`GDAL_CFLAGS` trio in this
recipe's `script_env`, which exists only to feed it.

--- a/setup.py
+++ b/setup.py
@@ -241,6 +241,9 @@

     def get_gdal_config(self, option):
         try:
+            var_name = f"GDAL_{option.upper()}"
+            if var_name in os.environ:
+                return os.environ[var_name]
             return fetch_config(option, gdal_config=self.gdal_config)
         except gdal_config_error:
             msg = 'Could not find gdal-config. Make sure you have installed the GDAL native library and development headers.'
